    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Sustainable Fashion – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Sustainable Fashion</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> What Is Sustainable Fashion?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Inform others about environmental or community issues. Interactive: Engage in discussions on global topics like environmental awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can inform peers about the impact of certain fashion practices (e.g., "Vintage clothes reduce waste because they are reused.")
                    Can engage in a discussion to explain how sustainable fashion benefits the environment (e.g., "Recycled fabrics will help conserve water and energy in the production process.")
                    Can discuss sustainable fashion trends and give opinions (e.g., "I believe more brands will prioritize sustainable materials in their designs to reduce environmental impact.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts the class by displaying two images: one showing a "fast fashion" store with many trendy, inexpensive clothes, and another showing a "vintage" or "eco-friendly" clothing store with unique, recycled items. Students are asked to briefly discuss in pairs: "Which store would you prefer to shop from and why? Use words like 'fashionable,' 'trendy,' 'eco-friendly,' or 'vintage'." (3-4 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models short sentences and phrases to initiate discussion, e.g., "I prefer to shop at 'vintage' stores because I like unique styles and it's more 'sustainable'." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /v/ in "vintage" and /w/ in "wear," and the stress in "fashionable" and "sustainability." For example, "Listen to me: /vɪntɪdʒ/ (vintage) vs. /wɛr/ (wear). Also, note the stress: fash-ion-able, sus-tain-a-bil-i-ty." (2 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of "sustainable" and "eco-friendly."
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "If something is 'sustainable,' does it help the environment or harm it?" (Expected answer: "It helps the environment.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "Is buying 'recycled' materials an 'eco-friendly' choice?" (Expected answer: "Yes, it is.")</li>
                    </ul> (2 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(7-8 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Sustainable Fashion".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short audio clip or a transcript of two teenagers discussing "What Is Sustainable Fashion?" and sharing predictions about future fashion trends. The input includes phrases using past continuous/present simple (e.g., "In the 90s, people were wearing baggy clothes. Today, tight styles are trendy."), past perfect (e.g., "By the 2010s, many brands had adopted eco-friendly materials."), and future simple (e.g., "I think fashion trends will continue to evolve towards sustainability."). Register is informal, like a peer conversation. (Audio clip ~1.5 minutes / Transcript ~150 words)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student is able to interact verbally to inform others about environmental or community issues and engage in discussions on global topics like environmental awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to/read the text and answer the following questions orally in pairs:
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gist Question: "What is the main topic the teenagers are discussing?" (Expected: Sustainable fashion and future trends.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: "One speaker believes fashion trends will stop evolving." (False)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Information Matching: "Which fashion period is associated with 'baggy clothes'?" (The 90s)</li>
                    </ul> The teacher facilitates a brief whole-class check of answers. (5 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(8-10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice speaking using past continuous with present simple to compare trends, past perfect for completed actions, and future simple for predictions, along with vocabulary (fashion, trend, vintage, eco-friendly, evolve, predict, recycle, etc.). Pragmatic function: Asking about fashion trends and discussing eco-friendly choices. Pronunciation: /v/ vs /w/, multisyllabic stress.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & Read Aloud):</b> Students receive sentences with blanks related to fashion history and sustainability. In pairs, they complete the sentences orally using target vocabulary and grammar, then read them aloud. E.g., "In the 80s, people _______ (wear) bold colors. Today, minimalist _______ (style) _______ (be) in vogue." (Expected: "were wearing," "styles are"). "By the time I was born, the fashion _______ (industry) _______ (already adopt) more diverse _______ (designs)." (Expected: "industry had already adopted more diverse designs."). Teacher circulates, providing feedback on accuracy and pronunciation, focusing on /v/ and /w/, and stress in "eco-friendly." (5 minutes)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled (Guided Questions & Discussion):</b> In pairs, students use provided sentence starters or questions to discuss fashion trends and sustainability. Teacher provides prompts like: "How have fashion trends evolved from the past to today?" "What impact had fast fashion had on the environment before sustainable alternatives became popular?" "What new eco-friendly materials do you predict brands will introduce in the future?" Students take turns asking and answering, ensuring to use past continuous/present simple, past perfect, and future simple. (5 minutes)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Opinion Sharing):</b> Students engage in a short conversation about personal fashion choices and sustainability. Prompts: "Do you prefer to shop for vintage clothes or new designs? Why?" "How can we encourage more people to adopt sustainable fashion practices?" "What do you think is the most important benefit of using recycled fabrics?" They practice organizing discussions and expressing opinions about sustainability. (5 minutes)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(15 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Sustainable Fashion".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Debate/Discussion. Students will work in groups of three. Each group will receive a prompt for a mini-debate related to sustainable fashion. Two students will present opposing viewpoints on the prompt, and the third student will act as a moderator, ensuring both speakers get a chance and asking clarifying questions. The task aims to inform peers about environmental issues and engage in discussions on global topics. The sociolinguistic objective is to reflect values and attitudes toward sustainability. (10 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Prompts (examples):</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Fast fashion is harmful and wasteful, and should be completely replaced by sustainable brands." vs. "Fast fashion is cheap and convenient, and can be improved with better regulations rather than being abolished."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Buying vintage clothes is the best way to be sustainable." vs. "New innovations in eco-friendly materials are more impactful for the future of fashion."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are encouraged to use the practiced grammar structures and vocabulary, discussing how fashion trends have evolved and what changes they predict for the future. The moderator uses pragmatic functions like "How have fashion trends evolved...?" or "What are your opinions on...?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes groups during the debate/discussion, noting students' participation, use of target language, and interaction skills. A simple rubric will be used to track individual and group performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' performance in the group debate/discussion from Stage 4. Each student's ability to express their ideas clearly, respond to peers, and integrate the target vocabulary and grammar will be assessed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /v/, /w/, and stress in multisyllabic words like "sustainability," "eco-friendly").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least three target vocabulary words (e.g., sustainable, vintage, recycle, evolve, predict, trend, eco-friendly).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target grammar structures (past continuous/present simple for comparison, past perfect for completed actions, future simple for predictions).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of ideas and ability to inform/discuss.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and engagement in the discussion.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I learned about sustainable fashion today is..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I feel confident discussing _______ (e.g., fashion trends, eco-friendly choices). I need more practice with _______ (e.g., using past perfect, predicting the future)."</li>
                    </ul> (5 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about discussing sustainable fashion with my peers, and how did I overcome it?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has my perspective on fashion and its environmental impact changed after today's discussion?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of the speaking activities worked well for most students? Did the students effectively use the past continuous/present simple, past perfect, and future simple to compare trends and make predictions? Were there any specific pronunciation challenges (e.g., /v/ vs /w/ or stress in "sustainability") that need further attention? What adjustments or additional scaffolding might be needed for students who struggled with fluency or expressing complex ideas about sustainability in English?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about sustainable fashion trends and their environmental impact. In Lesson 4, students will apply their understanding and vocabulary to write short descriptive paragraphs or opinion pieces about sustainable fashion, building on the ideas and arguments they developed today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>